00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:11.650
But first, I just want
to talk about what

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.650
HPC is for those of
you who don't know,

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:16.330
aren't familiar with it.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.910
So just some examples
of some industries

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.360
that HPC users
are in that really

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.910
is fundamental to
their industry--

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:27.460
manufacturing, so computer
aided design, chip design,

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:31.120
things like this, life
sciences, in terms of genomics,

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.380
financial services, climate
modeling, everything like that.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.960
So as you might know,
for those of you

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.480
who do HPC, in these industries,
compute is the real driver.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:46.840
So all the time questions
are getting bigger,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.370
demands for compute are growing.

00:01:48.370 --> 00:01:51.910
And so we see this, especially
in the top 500 supercomputers.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:56.290
So this is a graph of the growth
of those systems in FLOPS,

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.580
which is floating-point
operations per second.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:03.440
And so you can see that
we've grown now and exceeded,

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:06.170
in total capacity, an
exaFLOP of storage--

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.050
of performance-- I'm sorry.

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:11.530
And we're going to see the first
exascale system very shortly,

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.720
around 2021.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.690
So all of this compute--

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:20.810
and also storage is
constantly growing.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:24.320
So if you look at the last
10 years of storage growth,

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:26.410
we started in 2008 with
about three petabytes

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.305
on the largest system,
RoadRunner at Los Alamos.

00:02:29.305 --> 00:02:31.250
That was about 55
gigabytes a second,

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.930
which was pretty
impressive for the time.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:40.010
But in just 10 years, we've
83 x-ed the capacity to 250

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:43.960
petabytes in a single storage
system and gone up to 2 1/2

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.020
terabytes a second, which
is absolutely tremendous

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.570
the factors of scaling
that we're seeing,

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.670
not only in compute,
but also in storage.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.210
And so all of these things
together, combining compute,

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.790
and combining storage,
and software, of course,

00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:59.090
drives your mission.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.950
It's not just about
infrastructure.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.280
It's really about your science
and what you're trying to do.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:07.460
So a great example of that
is the Broad Institute.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.630
The Broad Institute is a
genomics research organization

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.660
and genomic sequencing
organization in partnership

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.520
with MIT, and
Harvard, and others.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:19.210
And so these guys
were able to, using

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.780
Google Cloud and the mass amount
of compute that they have,

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:27.080
get the price of processing a
single genome down to under $5,

00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:30.010
which is really tremendous
for making genome sequencing

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:32.440
accessible to the world
and letting you do things

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:33.800
like 23andMe, and so on.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.440
And so when you
think about what it

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.830
takes to actually do this work,
it really costs, first of all,

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:43.610
a lot of money.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:46.300
So that's why you saw
national laboratories owned

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.450
these systems.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.510
To build these systems
at a massive scale

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.210
is really a huge undertaking,
not only in infrastructure,

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:56.800
but also in buildings,
and maintenance,

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.010
and everything like that.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:01.390
But it's also very difficult to
run these systems efficiently.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.490
So you can see on the right a
graph of typical utilization

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.580
in a system.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.550
And blue is the total
capacity of your system,

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:11.380
where the red is jobs.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.390
And so at a time of
very high utilization,

00:04:13.390 --> 00:04:16.180
you have users sitting in the
queue waiting for their jobs

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.029
to run waiting for
other jobs to complete.

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:20.790
And on the right,
you can see times

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.290
when there aren't enough jobs,
and you're just wasting money

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.480
running your supercomputer.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.330
And so this is a problem
even within Google.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.160
But we've gotten
really, really good

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.940
at bin packing,
which is a term used

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.620
in HPC, which basically means
just utilizing your resources

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:37.600
very efficiently.

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.250
And so that's all
driven by Borg.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.680
And so we'll talk a
little bit about how

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.470
we do this in Google Cloud.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.260
And so to avoid having
to deal with all

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:50.960
of this on your
own system on prem,

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.760
you can really very easily
turn to Google Cloud for all

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.770
of our very easy,
fast provisioning,

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.440
our security, and our storage,
which we'll be talking

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.810
about a little bit more.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:04.660
And so another user that
decided that it would probably

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.610
be a good idea to turn
to Google was Twitter.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.690
And so you'll probably
hear at our keynotes

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:14.140
this week that Twitter
moved 300 petabytes of data

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.180
to Google Cloud Storage.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.850
And they used Google Cloud and
decided on Google Cloud because

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:20.890
of all the reasons
that I just showed you

00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:25.810
in terms of faster provisioning,
flexibility, and security.

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.870
So in terms of infrastructure
and what it actually

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.580
takes to run HPC--

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:33.640
very quickly before we dive
deeper into the HPC storage--

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:35.200
you really need three things.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.250
That's compute, storage,
and networking, right?

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:43.270
So if we look at compute,
the fundamental driver

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.560
of compute in Google Cloud
is the Google Compute Engine.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.670
And so Compute Engine
is really designed

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:51.590
to be powerful and flexible
for your workloads.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:54.740
So we have up to 416
CPUs in a single machine,

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.270
12 terabytes of RAM, which you
just heard about this week.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:01.270
We have Intel Optane
and local SSDs,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.110
which we'll talk
about in a little bit.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:04.485
But we also have
something that's

00:06:04.485 --> 00:06:08.230
very key to HPC workloads
called custom machine types.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.540
And so this is a
way that you can

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.312
size your instance,
choosing very specifically

00:06:13.312 --> 00:06:14.770
the number of CPUs
and RAM that you

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:18.100
want to perfectly fit
every workload that you

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.010
run on Google Compute Engine.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.440
So if you have one
system that needs

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:30.350
12 terabytes of RAM and 400
CPUs and GPUs, you can do that.

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:33.730
If you need one that needs 12
CPUs and 12 gigabytes of Ram,

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:36.270
you can build one.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:40.570
We also have compute optimized
VMs, which are super high speed

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:44.010
CPU machines great for HPC.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:45.150
We have GPUs, of course.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:50.070
So we have Invidia K80s
all the way through T4s,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.920
as well as TPUs, which
are awesome for machine

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.230
learning use cases.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:58.350
Preemptible VMs
are an awesome tool

00:06:58.350 --> 00:07:01.980
for running check-pointed
and quickly running code.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.920
So they are up to 80% off
of a standard instance.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.490
However they live for
a maximum of 24 hours

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.040
and can get preempted
anytime within that 24 hours

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.350
with 30 seconds of notice.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:16.980
So again, check-pointed jobs,
jobs like genomic sequencing

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.140
where a single job
within a batch of jobs

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:24.060
can fail and be restarted is
perfect for preemptible VMs.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:27.900
And then at the instance
level, networking scales,

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:29.550
in terms of bandwidth,
at two gigabits

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:34.620
per second per vCPU up to 16
gigabits per second per vCPU.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.860
And we support
15,000 VMs per VPC.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.160
So that's a lot of instances
on a single network.

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:44.700
If you need more than
that, let me know.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.160
And speaking of networking,
in terms of the global network

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:50.040
that you need for massive
collaboration and massive data

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:53.350
storage, we have exactly that.

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:56.450
So we've invested billions
of dollars into our network.

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.200
And when you think
about a global network,

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:00.117
you have to think about
laying undersea cables

00:08:00.117 --> 00:08:03.840
and dealing with things
like shark attacks, which

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:06.120
sharks like the electromagnetic
signals coming off

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:06.720
of the wires.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.880
I don't know why.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.680
So we have a
shark-proof network.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.180
It's just one benefit.

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:18.410
And then finally, in
terms of compute storage,

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:21.430
we have a wide array of
local compute storage

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.430
options in terms of not only
Google Cloud Storage for object

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:26.380
storage, but also
persistent disk,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:30.180
local storage like local
SSD, and then Filestore

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.950
and other solutions that
we'll be talking about just

00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:35.720
in a little bit.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:40.690
So to talk about HPC storage in
Google Cloud at a larger scale,

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:44.020
I'd like to introduce Dean.

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:47.050
DEAN HILDEBRAND:
Great, thanks Wyatt.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:50.450
So what I wanted to talk
a little bit about is--

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:54.100
all right, now we're thinking
about doing HPC in the cloud.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:58.888
In many cases, users already
have an HPC infrastructure,

00:08:58.888 --> 00:09:00.680
like a compute-- you
know, a Linux cluster,

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:04.060
or a supercomputer, or something
in their existing premise.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.760
They're not just coming
to this brand new.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:09.400
But in a lot of cases,
that can happen, too.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:12.640
So what you end up with is
a series of on-premise data

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:14.470
centers, each with
their own cluster,

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:18.400
and then connected into
a variety of Google Cloud

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:19.460
regions.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:22.780
And so the trick then is how
do we get data into the regions

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:23.620
and back again?

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.390
And then once we
have the region up

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.340
and running, how do we
tailor make, I like to say,

00:09:30.340 --> 00:09:33.190
the compute side and the
storage side for the workload

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:36.220
that you're trying to run?

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:38.310
So some of the common
models that we're

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:42.570
seeing from a
variety of HPC users

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:45.340
boils down into
three categories.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:47.610
One of them is what I
like to call launch, which

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:50.550
is you're running maybe
Matlab or some smaller

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:54.307
program on your laptop or
on a desktop somewhere.

00:09:54.307 --> 00:09:55.890
And you're not using
a lot of compute,

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.360
because it's just
a single machine

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.770
or a couple of machines.

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:02.620
But then you want to dynamically
burst your job into the cloud.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.110
And so there are
partners that can

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:08.610
enable that where you're
using two cores here,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:11.790
let's say, and you burst
out to 200 something.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.980
And it becomes a very
dynamic real time

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:16.860
type bursting scenario.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.580
Good for bursting compute, not
so good for bursting storage--

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:23.438
because if you're trying
to do this in real time,

00:10:23.438 --> 00:10:25.230
you're going to wait
for that data transfer

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:30.030
to end up happening while you're
trying to dynamically burst.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.420
The one that I see
a little more common

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.430
is the burst by
type, I like to say,

00:10:35.430 --> 00:10:38.070
which is essentially
taking the workloads that

00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:40.140
are running on premise
and splitting them up

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:41.760
into a set of categories.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:46.080
And so two categories might be
the HPC high performance type

00:10:46.080 --> 00:10:49.740
workloads that require
super low latency networks

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.890
and use MPI and a variety
of those kinds of tools,

00:10:53.890 --> 00:10:56.370
and then separating that
out from the HTC, so

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.640
the high throughput computing,
which is more embarrassingly

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.902
parallel type jobs or
ensembles in some communities

00:11:04.902 --> 00:11:06.360
where they're
running just millions

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:08.500
of smaller jobs on the system.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:13.200
And so what you can do is then
leave the on premise system

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:16.170
that you have, optimize
that for one set of jobs--

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:18.420
let's say the HPC type jobs--

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.430
and then burst the
rest into the cloud,

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:23.040
and have the data stored
permanently up in the cloud.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.190
And so you're really sort of
bifurcating your data plane,

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:27.330
but as well as your jobs.

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:28.800
And then that way
that enables you

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.140
to optimize-- instead of
having to optimize on premise

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:35.790
for every workload, small
IOPs and big bandwidth jobs,

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.775
you can really separate and
target each one individually.

00:11:38.775 --> 00:11:40.150
And then the last
one, of course,

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:44.040
is all in, which is
obviously awesome and removes

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.510
the hassle of trying to do
data movement back and forth.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:50.408
But I think that that's
more of a process in terms

00:11:50.408 --> 00:11:52.200
of getting there all
the way to that phase.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:58.770
So on the storage side,
then, what you end up with

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.900
is an on premise
data center that

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:04.470
probably has your Linux cluster
running and your own storage

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:05.160
system.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.250
And then you have a
set of file systems

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.490
that you might want to
target inside the cloud.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:14.080
So here, just as an example,
I have our Google Cloud

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:14.580
Filestore.

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:18.790
And then we have a Lustre
system in the system.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:21.870
And so first you can
see that we can tailor

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:25.920
make the compute side of
each of these clusters

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.570
to whatever the workload is.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:31.050
So Filestore is a smaller
NFS based offering.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.120
So you'd have a
smaller set of nodes

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.110
that would be accessing that
with whatever number of CPUs

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.170
that they require,
and tailor making then

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:43.723
the storage in Filestore for
whatever the requirements are.

00:12:43.723 --> 00:12:45.390
And then we have the
other one, as well,

00:12:45.390 --> 00:12:48.360
on the larger compute cluster
that you're going to have,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:52.260
and then targeting a Lustre
system that's optimized

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:53.810
for whatever the workload is--

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.782
high bandwidth, high IOPs,
or whatever is needed.

00:12:57.782 --> 00:12:59.490
And then the challenge
becomes how do you

00:12:59.490 --> 00:13:00.960
get the data back and forth?

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.980
And so a lot of cases people
are using Cloud storage

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:07.230
in the middle for
that data transfer.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:11.310
Because it provides the
secure and high bandwidth

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:14.670
pipe that you basically need
in order to get the data up.

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:18.000
And then from cloud storage, you
can then essentially burst out

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:19.890
into your file systems.

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.630
And then the results
can then flow back

00:13:21.630 --> 00:13:22.760
the other way, as needed.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.860
So talking a little bit about
our Cloud Storage offering--

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:31.300
I think most of you might
already know about it--

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.940
but essentially, it can
be used for a variety

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:34.880
of different purposes.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.550
You can use it as your
production HPC storage

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:40.390
offering.

00:13:40.390 --> 00:13:44.720
Or you can also just use it
as a cold tire and store,

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:47.110
essentially, the cold
data in your system

00:13:47.110 --> 00:13:49.803
and save money by
keeping it there.

00:13:49.803 --> 00:13:51.970
Or you can just use it as,
again, a transitory place

00:13:51.970 --> 00:13:55.210
to copy data in and out as
data is flowing back and forth

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.770
from your on premise system.

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:01.450
And so Cloud Storage
is obviously awesome.

00:14:01.450 --> 00:14:02.970
And it can do amazing things.

00:14:02.970 --> 00:14:05.380
And it can scale
across the globe.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:09.270
But in many cases what
you end up with is still

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:11.243
your applications are
not written for that.

00:14:11.243 --> 00:14:12.660
And if you're
writing applications

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.720
for on premise supercomputers
or Linux clusters,

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:17.940
you probably are not
writing applications

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:19.960
for object storage,
in which case,

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:23.820
you end up with needing
some sort of file storage.

00:14:23.820 --> 00:14:26.310
And so I ask this
question, well,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.152
what do you mean, file
systems in the cloud?

00:14:28.152 --> 00:14:29.610
You know, we're
going to modernize.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.290
This old file system
stuff, you know,

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:33.480
POSIX is from 50 years ago.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.800
Like why are we there?

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:39.900
But the thing is, as I
said, many HPC workflows

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:44.340
that are running today rely
on a POSIX based file system.

00:14:44.340 --> 00:14:46.230
They rely on the performance.

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:50.340
And they also rely on
the types of behaviors

00:14:50.340 --> 00:14:54.390
that file storage can support,
such as in place updates,

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:56.850
handling large numbers
of small files,

00:14:56.850 --> 00:15:01.050
because they deliver latencies
that are sub-millisecond versus

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:07.530
the tens of milliseconds that
object cloud storage provides.

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.990
And the other thing that
I like to remind people

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:13.080
is that file is actually
very cloud friendly.

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.520
NFS says the concept
is built into Linux.

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.150
And it's available from
every VM that you launch.

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:21.750
And it's a core part of the
Kubernetes infrastructure.

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:25.650
And you can dynamically
use NFS in Kubernetes

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.940
just without doing
any work whatsoever.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:31.350
And so it's also very
friendly to preemptible

00:15:31.350 --> 00:15:35.250
VMs, which is another core part
of HPC, as Wyatt brought up.

00:15:35.250 --> 00:15:37.560
And obviously, very
familiar, right-- all of us

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:38.880
understand what file is.

00:15:38.880 --> 00:15:41.320
We do this on our
laptops every day.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:45.060
These are the apps that we
learned to write in university.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.820
And so it's just
something that becomes

00:15:47.820 --> 00:15:50.740
a very natural transition.

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:53.670
So what I wanted to do then
is go into some of the details

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:54.170
that--

00:15:54.170 --> 00:15:56.000
OK, so you're going
to use the cloud.

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:00.120
You're going to deploy a larger
file system inside the cloud.

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.840
Going through some of the
just general bare bones

00:16:03.840 --> 00:16:08.200
ways on how GCP works in
terms of compute and storage--

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:13.020
so at the top there, we
have our compute VMs.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:15.720
And so something,
as Wyatt said, you

00:16:15.720 --> 00:16:19.350
need to choose the
right number of vCPUs,

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:22.680
or if you're GPUs or TPUs--
whatever the infrastructure you

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:24.540
need for your compute layer.

00:16:24.540 --> 00:16:27.810
But then these nodes are
also your file system client,

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:32.490
whether it's NFS or a POSIX
based client such as Lustre.

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.290
And so you need to
make sure that it

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.640
has the bandwidth it needs.

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:38.683
And so at the two
gigabits per vCPU,

00:16:38.683 --> 00:16:41.100
you always need to make sure
that you have enough of those

00:16:41.100 --> 00:16:43.590
to at least deliver the
network performance.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:45.090
Even if you didn't
need them, you're

00:16:45.090 --> 00:16:48.390
going to need them for
the network, I'm sure.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.900
So the next step, then,
is the storage servers.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.660
So these file system
clients are then

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:57.630
using some level
of distributed IO

00:16:57.630 --> 00:17:00.150
across a set of storage servers
that you're going to launch.

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:03.870
And these end up being VMs
inside of Google Cloud,

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:04.960
as well.

00:17:04.960 --> 00:17:07.079
So you need to keep the
same things in mind.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:08.246
The one is the networking.

00:17:08.246 --> 00:17:10.079
There's no point in
putting up a whole bunch

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:12.520
of small VMs in many cases.

00:17:12.520 --> 00:17:14.520
You're going to want to
have the largest network

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:16.079
capacity possible.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:18.720
So you're going to want
to think about at least

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:23.170
eight cores for those servers.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:25.410
And you might then want
to think about, well,

00:17:25.410 --> 00:17:28.800
does the file system support
some level of extra caching

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:31.740
where we could put
in some local SSDs?

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:33.750
How much RAM do we
want to support?

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.430
And all of that
can be configurable

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:39.660
depending on the type of
job that you're using.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:41.190
So then finally,
there's going to be

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:44.350
some storage, would be the
point of the file system.

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:46.980
And so if you're going to
store this data durably,

00:17:46.980 --> 00:17:49.410
you're going to be
using persistent disk.

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.110
And then there's a few things
that you need to watch out

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:53.920
there for, as well.

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:59.550
And so one of them is that the
performance scales linearly

00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:01.560
with capacity.

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.332
So you can look at your
capacity requirements.

00:18:04.332 --> 00:18:06.790
And then you have to look at
your performance requirements.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:08.957
And then you need to choose
the capacity-- provision

00:18:08.957 --> 00:18:11.350
the capacity that
meets both of those.

00:18:11.350 --> 00:18:14.020
So some people don't like
paying for extra capacity

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.270
if you're not going to use it.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.830
But don't think of it that way.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:19.170
Think of it as you're
paying for performance.

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:21.030
And some of that
is just logical.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:23.460
I mean you can't have--

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:28.050
the more capacity it has, the
more spindles or the more SSDs

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.820
you're engaging,
which means then you

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:33.780
have-- enables more bandwidth
and IOPs into the system.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:39.070
So it works very similarly onto
how on premise systems work.

00:18:39.070 --> 00:18:43.260
The other thing to remember
is that with PD-SSD there

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.440
is a set of vCPU requirements.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:49.620
And that's because the
high IOP of using SSDs just

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:50.970
drives cores.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.820
And so it's a tiered system.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:57.060
But you get the best
performance with using 32 vCPUs

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:58.410
at that level.

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:00.960
And then the other
thing is to remember

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.240
is that they scale linearly.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.430
But then they hit different
limits at different capacities.

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:09.030
And so it's easier just to
choose the largest capacity.

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:11.520
And then you don't have to
worry about whether or not

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:14.280
you could somehow have
eked out performance.

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:16.350
But some of the common
mistakes I've seen

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.680
is where they choose smaller
VMs and smaller capacities,

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.340
and then they have
a lot of them.

00:19:21.340 --> 00:19:23.430
And it's just a lot
easier to shrink

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:26.700
the number of VMs and storage
servers that you're deploying

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:30.210
and maximize out the bandwidth
and the IOPs of each of them.

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:37.550
All right, so we have a lot
of storage options in Google.

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:41.390
And it can be quite confusing
in terms of figuring out

00:19:41.390 --> 00:19:43.920
which ones are right for you.

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:48.180
The top row here is the
structured storage options,

00:19:48.180 --> 00:19:49.430
which I always like to put up.

00:19:49.430 --> 00:19:52.260
But we're focusing in on
the bottom ones today.

00:19:52.260 --> 00:19:54.350
And so we talked a little
bit about Cloud Storage

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:56.110
and Persistent Disk.

00:19:56.110 --> 00:19:57.680
And I'll mention
Filestore briefly.

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:00.950
But then we also have a
set of HPC storage partners

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:04.400
that we're partnering
with and are putting up

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:06.890
different levels of file
systems inside of Google

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:12.190
that are really targeted
towards these HPC use cases.

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:15.960
So the first one to talk about
is Google's own Filestore.

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:20.310
So this is great for small to
medium sized jobs, like I said,

00:20:20.310 --> 00:20:27.000
maybe a Matlab job or some
sort of smaller test system

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.880
that you're running.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:34.360
And it's fully managed
and NFS based from Google.

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:36.730
Next, if you need to
go bigger than that,

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:40.930
elastifile is putting up a
fully managed NFS space scale

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:44.110
out file system that
really is targeted

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:48.130
as the next tier of
Filestore and delivers

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:49.850
a really great number of IOPs.

00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:51.760
So if you have a
high IOP workload,

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:54.170
then this is a great choice.

00:20:54.170 --> 00:20:57.490
And of course, we have our
Netapp Cloud Volume Service.

00:20:57.490 --> 00:21:00.010
And so with them you
get all the performance

00:21:00.010 --> 00:21:03.940
that you normally expect out
of using Netapp on premise.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:06.040
But you get it with
all of the integration

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:08.500
and the ease of use of GCP.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:11.650
And so it's just
like the other two.

00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:15.412
It's fully integrated into the
console and the billing system.

00:21:15.412 --> 00:21:16.870
And you can just
dynamically use it

00:21:16.870 --> 00:21:19.420
as if you were using
any of the other storage

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:22.250
options in Google.

00:21:22.250 --> 00:21:25.600
And so we are also developing
now a partnership with DDN

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:28.420
around their Lustre service and
their other software storage

00:21:28.420 --> 00:21:29.390
offerings.

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:32.770
And so now I'm going to
pass it off to Sven from DDN

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:35.342
to talk about that.

00:21:35.342 --> 00:21:37.050
SVEN OEHME: Hello, my
