And then we're going
to look into this.

00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:09.640
And so if you deploy the
system, as Minh said,

00:26:09.640 --> 00:26:12.670
this is probably going to take
somewhere around four minutes.

00:26:12.670 --> 00:26:15.880
We also deployed large systems
of hundreds of terabytes

00:26:15.880 --> 00:26:16.420
in size.

00:26:16.420 --> 00:26:17.920
And everything is
basically a matter

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:19.970
of a couple of minutes
to get deployed.

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.760
So it's actually-- it's pretty
easy and pretty scalable

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:27.080
if you need a very large amount
of capacity in the cloud.

00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:30.190
So let's talk a little
bit more about Lustre

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:33.590
and what DDN is actually
bringing to the table here.

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:36.850
So DDN's Lustre is the number
world [INAUDIBLE] system

00:26:36.850 --> 00:26:38.030
deployed.

00:26:38.030 --> 00:26:40.070
We have a large number
of AI customers.

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:41.530
So we're not just
playing in HPC.

00:26:41.530 --> 00:26:46.640
We have very specific offerings
in the AI space, as well.

00:26:46.640 --> 00:26:48.640
And the whole purpose
of Lustre is really

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:51.340
to provide very,
very high performance

00:26:51.340 --> 00:26:54.280
where traditional file serving
solutions typically lack in.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:57.700
So why we can do a lot of
things with NFS and SMP

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:59.260
with normal workloads--
as soon as we

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:01.270
get to massive amount
of parallelism,

00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:04.070
they are typically not really
your choice of workloads.

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:07.160
So we have some customers
today deployed in the world--

00:27:07.160 --> 00:27:09.430
if you look at the top
500 supercomputing list,

00:27:09.430 --> 00:27:12.270
a massive amount of them are
actually running the Lustre

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:13.660
file system inside.

00:27:13.660 --> 00:27:17.620
So there is a very interesting
performance benchmark

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:18.520
called IO 500.

00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:20.320
I'm talking a little
bit more about that.

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:21.778
And what we see
here is essentially

00:27:21.778 --> 00:27:25.010
it demonstrates the
capabilities of that offering.

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:28.180
So we have customers that have
millions or billions of files.

00:27:28.180 --> 00:27:30.610
We can do hundreds of
gigabytes, even terabytes

00:27:30.610 --> 00:27:32.920
per second throughput
with this technology.

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:35.050
It's something that is
really provably deployed

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:36.460
in many, many sites.

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:39.880
So we basically try to bring
this on prem technology

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:41.400
that we deploy in
many, many sites

00:27:41.400 --> 00:27:44.740
today in the cloud to
give customers the choice

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:47.350
to not just run this on prem,
but also get a very, very

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:50.710
nice cloud experience with a
very proven technology that

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:53.950
is deployed in many
of these areas today.

00:27:53.950 --> 00:27:56.930
So the things that
primarily differentiate

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.380
Lustre from some of
these other options

00:27:59.380 --> 00:28:02.740
that you see on the
marketplace is really

00:28:02.740 --> 00:28:05.090
superiority and scalability.

00:28:05.090 --> 00:28:07.750
As I just said, deploying
hundreds of millions,

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:10.510
even billions of files,
petabyte size systems--

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:12.640
we have some systems
on prem today

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:15.470
that are three digits
petabytes in size.

00:28:15.470 --> 00:28:17.170
So we know that the
technology scales.

00:28:17.170 --> 00:28:20.710
So we can deploy this in the
cloud with the same technology.

00:28:20.710 --> 00:28:23.230
The other nice thing is that
you can very nicely scale it

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:25.430
for various types of workloads.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:28.330
It doesn't matter if you have
massive random read performance

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:31.770
workloads or you have massively
sequential workloads for reads

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:32.740
over writes.

00:28:32.740 --> 00:28:34.410
We have small files
or large files.

00:28:34.410 --> 00:28:36.550
There is lots of options
that you can have.

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:38.690
We try to keep it as
simple as possible.

00:28:38.690 --> 00:28:40.870
That's why we have all
these profiles where you

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:42.323
can basically pre-select from.

00:28:42.323 --> 00:28:43.990
But if you need
something that is highly

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:45.730
tailored for your
environment, you

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:47.930
can do this with the
offering, as well.

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:50.770
And what a lot of people don't
know is many AI workloads,

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:54.270
particularly-- and we really put
lots of effort in this area--

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:57.340
rely under the cover on mmap().

00:28:57.340 --> 00:29:00.400
So many, many libraries,
LMDB and multiple others,

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:02.200
use mmap() under the cover.

00:29:02.200 --> 00:29:04.060
Many other file system
options actually

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:07.250
have significant performance
scaling issues with that.

00:29:07.250 --> 00:29:09.970
And so we put lots of
investment into this area

00:29:09.970 --> 00:29:11.870
to make this very, very fast.

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:14.530
So we believe that's why this
option is very interesting,

00:29:14.530 --> 00:29:18.240
particularly if you try to
run AI workloads in the cloud.

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:21.010
Because with a distributed file
system that has optimizations

00:29:21.010 --> 00:29:23.650
for that, a lot of the
frameworks that basically get

00:29:23.650 --> 00:29:26.350
shipped out of the box on
these systems are going to run

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.410
significantly faster if
they leverage mmap() under

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:32.300
the cover.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:35.910
We also have-- given the size of
the deployments we already had,

00:29:35.910 --> 00:29:38.560
there is lots of expertise
in the organization

00:29:38.560 --> 00:29:41.620
to manage massively
scalable systems.

00:29:41.620 --> 00:29:44.350
We actually run for lots
of customers very massively

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:45.760
scaled systems on site.

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:49.930
So we provide these extra
services to customers on prem.

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:52.750
And basically, with the service
offering and the knowledge

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:55.390
that we have in this area,
we try to bring all of that

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:56.643
into the cloud, as well.

00:29:56.643 --> 00:29:58.060
And we are more
than happy to also

00:29:58.060 --> 00:30:00.580
have separate engagements
in specialized services

00:30:00.580 --> 00:30:03.770
for that if there are very
specific needs you have.

00:30:03.770 --> 00:30:06.040
So the technology is
heavily being worked on.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:08.260
We tried to tailor, as
I said, this offering

00:30:08.260 --> 00:30:09.870
specifically for cloud.

00:30:09.870 --> 00:30:11.865
There is lots of
powerful tools that we

00:30:11.865 --> 00:30:15.040
are in the process of launching
in subsequent releases that

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:17.700
are going to come over the
next couple of quarters.

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:20.800
So you have, today, already
capabilities to set a quota

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:22.300
allocation in the system.

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:24.250
You can either do
this by sub-directory,

00:30:24.250 --> 00:30:27.320
there is options you can do
this by user or by group.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:29.620
And we going to add
significant management

00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:31.720
capabilities in
terms of pooling.

00:30:31.720 --> 00:30:34.000
So we have much more
fine-grained control as an end

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:37.450
user or based on data that the
system automatically collects

00:30:37.450 --> 00:30:41.380
in terms of file heat and being
able to basically manage data

00:30:41.380 --> 00:30:44.920
placement across multiple pools
of different types of storage

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:47.860
inside the system at basically--

00:30:47.860 --> 00:30:50.665
with the full automatism and
the tools required for the end

00:30:50.665 --> 00:30:52.020
users to manage that.

00:30:52.020 --> 00:30:55.570
We also have a capability
for doing very fast transfers

00:30:55.570 --> 00:30:57.448
from on prem to cloud.

00:30:57.448 --> 00:30:59.740
Because it's great if you
have an offering on the cloud

00:30:59.740 --> 00:31:01.365
or if you have an
offering on the prem.

00:31:01.365 --> 00:31:03.580
But if you can't figure out
how to make the movement

00:31:03.580 --> 00:31:06.420
for these very, very large
scale systems working,

00:31:06.420 --> 00:31:07.420
you need more than that.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:09.003
And we have a special
product, which I

00:31:09.003 --> 00:31:10.960
have one extra slide for that.

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:13.450
And there's auditing
capabilities, as well,

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:16.150
that is required, obviously,
for lots of customers

00:31:16.150 --> 00:31:19.000
for governance purposes.

00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:21.880
And we have lots of security
features into this solution,

00:31:21.880 --> 00:31:22.630
as well.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:24.460
So you can do things
like secure mounts

00:31:24.460 --> 00:31:27.220
where people only can mount
certain sub-directories

00:31:27.220 --> 00:31:30.820
of the system without other
people essentially seeing

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:32.240
the data of each other.

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.635
So we can basically separate
each other's out in the file

00:31:35.635 --> 00:31:38.330
[INAUDIBLE] namespace.

00:31:38.330 --> 00:31:42.430
So IO500, as I said,
it's a benchmark

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:45.490
that is used trying
to provide, basically,

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:48.520
a very fair comparison between
different storage solutions

00:31:48.520 --> 00:31:50.420
in the market out there.

00:31:50.420 --> 00:31:56.460
We've done a couple of tests
in the IO500 benchmark.

00:31:56.460 --> 00:32:01.780
With Lustre, this is just one
example of what we deployed.

00:32:01.780 --> 00:32:06.720
We basically deployed 120 client
system, 120 service system,

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.900
with 180 terabyte
capacity in total.

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.550
We achieved 107 gigabytes
per second throughput

00:32:12.550 --> 00:32:13.570
on the system.

00:32:13.570 --> 00:32:16.840
And we basically showed
absolute linear scalability

00:32:16.840 --> 00:32:18.490
going from the
smallest configuration

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:20.260
we've tested all the way up.

00:32:20.260 --> 00:32:22.298
And as Minh already
mentioned, and what

00:32:22.298 --> 00:32:24.340
we're going to show in
the demo in a few seconds,

00:32:24.340 --> 00:32:26.650
is that all of this was
launched, essentially,

00:32:26.650 --> 00:32:30.520
and executed in a
couple of minutes.

00:32:30.520 --> 00:32:33.080
These are the results
of the benchmarks.

00:32:33.080 --> 00:32:34.570
So we did different tests.

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:37.340
So we scaled service
independently of client.

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:41.150
So this is essentially the
Lustre server count scaling.

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:44.410
So you see the IO500
score, how it basically

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:46.330
goes up when we add servers.

00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:48.310
I have to mention one
point-- the benchmark

00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:50.150
itself is very complicated.

00:32:50.150 --> 00:32:52.990
So as large you make the
benchmark data size as more

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:55.630
complex its execution is.

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:58.540
And so as higher you go,
basically, in the scores,

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:00.940
the tougher it's going
to be if you increase

00:33:00.940 --> 00:33:02.830
the scale of the system.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:07.090
And we also, as I said,
increased the gigabytes

00:33:07.090 --> 00:33:09.700
per second, essentially by
just adding individual nodes

00:33:09.700 --> 00:33:10.970
into the system.

00:33:10.970 --> 00:33:13.540
So here you see the
scale basically going

00:33:13.540 --> 00:33:17.140
from 10 servers, to 60
servers, to 120 servers.

00:33:17.140 --> 00:33:20.560
And you see basically a very
nice scale of that performance

00:33:20.560 --> 00:33:21.610
in the system.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:24.520
And we believe if you just add
more nodes into the system,

00:33:24.520 --> 00:33:28.030
it's just basically going to
run faster and faster as more

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.160
resources you add into this.

00:33:30.160 --> 00:33:31.870
So the nice thing
is, as I said, you

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:35.830
can scale capacity completely
independent from metadata

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:39.350
and independent of the IOPs
or throughput in the system.

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:41.920
So basically whatever
metrics you try to achieve,

00:33:41.920 --> 00:33:44.860
it's just basically a matter
of picking the right input

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:46.220
for the deployment.

00:33:46.220 --> 00:33:50.420
And we can basically
meet all of those needs.

00:33:50.420 --> 00:33:52.540
So two more slides
that are very specific.

00:33:52.540 --> 00:33:55.540
I said this is our
cloud offering.

00:33:55.540 --> 00:33:58.330
Lots of our customers already
have on prem offering.

00:33:58.330 --> 00:34:00.400
Or customers that
have a cloud offering

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:02.360
also need an on prem offering.

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:04.360
So I want to show you
two slides of what

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:06.758
we deploy to the on prem.

00:34:06.758 --> 00:34:08.300
So we've just launched
a new product.

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.420
It's called SFA18K.

00:34:10.420 --> 00:34:13.389
It's essentially the
fastest storage controller

00:34:13.389 --> 00:34:15.969
for file systems that you
can purchase right now.

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:17.679
It's essentially
a 4U device that

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:21.889
delivers 80 gigabytes per second
throughput over the network.

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:25.539
And this is essentially embedded
running the same Lustre file

00:34:25.539 --> 00:34:28.690
system software that
we deploy on GCP.

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:30.460
So if you're looking
for an offering that

00:34:30.460 --> 00:34:31.989
needs to be deployed
on prem, this

00:34:31.989 --> 00:34:34.540
is a very, very good
offering for that.

00:34:34.540 --> 00:34:36.639
And the second slide
that I wanted to show you

00:34:36.639 --> 00:34:38.332
is a product called Dataflow.

00:34:38.332 --> 00:34:39.790
So this is the one
that essentially

00:34:39.790 --> 00:34:44.260
ties this on prem technology
with the cloud technology,

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:48.585
or the cloud to cloud,
or on prem to on prem.

00:34:48.585 --> 00:34:49.960
So the product's
called Dataflow.

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:52.120
And what Dataflow
essentially does,

00:34:52.120 --> 00:34:55.239
it tightly integrates with
multiple storage sources

00:34:55.239 --> 00:34:56.260
and targets.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.770
So it's not actually limited to
just Lustre on prem and Lustre

00:34:59.770 --> 00:35:00.910
on the cloud.

00:35:00.910 --> 00:35:02.500
It has targets to NFS.

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:04.270
It has targets to SMB.

00:35:04.270 --> 00:35:07.300
It has targets to IBM
Spectrum Scale file system.

00:35:07.300 --> 00:35:10.230
It has targets to
multiple different sources

00:35:10.230 --> 00:35:14.530
and targets including S3, and
even has support for Tape.

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:16.420
And what differentiates
this technology

00:35:16.420 --> 00:35:20.170
from other simple file movement
or data management solutions

00:35:20.170 --> 00:35:23.570
is that it's particularly
built for massive scale.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:26.740
So you can basically take
a 100 petabytes system

00:35:26.740 --> 00:35:29.110
as a source and a multi--

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:30.600
hundreds of terabytes
or petabytes

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:33.460
file system in the cloud.

00:35:33.460 --> 00:35:35.950
And you can basically
fan out the data movement

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:38.945
across large number of nodes
and large number of networks.

00:35:38.945 --> 00:35:40.570
So instead of running
on a single node,

00:35:40.570 --> 00:35:44.050
it's essentially going to send
a request to the source system.

00:35:44.050 --> 00:35:47.380
You specify with rules what
data you want to be moved.

00:35:47.380 --> 00:35:49.450
It finds this data
very fast with built

00:35:49.450 --> 00:35:52.430
in technologies in that
technology if it's supported.

00:35:52.430 --> 00:35:52.930
So

00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:55.450
For example, if you run this
against a Spectrum Scale

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:57.910
cluster from IBM, it's
going to use the policy

00:35:57.910 --> 00:35:59.780
engine that is built in there.

00:35:59.780 --> 00:36:02.770
It's going to find the files
very quickly that you select,

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.710
then splits the workload across
large number of workloads

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:07.973
and transfers them in
parallel into the cloud.

00:36:07.973 --> 00:36:09.640
Because that's one
of the big challenges

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:11.810
with these massive
data movements.

00:36:11.810 --> 00:36:14.410
How do you move that data
between cloud and on prem.

00:36:14.410 --> 00:36:16.720
And you can't really do
this from a single node

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:20.620
you need a massively distributed
system in order to do that.

00:36:20.620 --> 00:36:24.280
And basically Dataflow
is providing you

00:36:24.280 --> 00:36:27.160
exactly that capability
to move between all

00:36:27.160 --> 00:36:29.440
these different technologies.

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:32.050
MINH DIEP: OK, so
there was the screen

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:35.020
last we left when
it finished deploy

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:36.670
approximately four
to five minute

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:39.980
on this particular profile.

00:36:39.980 --> 00:36:44.930
So as you can see on
the right hand side,

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:47.350
you see some information.

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:54.190
What we provide is a web
console that you can see file--

00:36:54.190 --> 00:36:56.440
you can see your statistic.

00:36:56.440 --> 00:36:58.825
Or you can see status of
your Lustre file system.

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:06.400
So this will display the Lustre
network, file system name,

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:13.550
also the NID, the network
LNET NID that you use.

00:37:13.550 --> 00:37:17.620
If you have new client
bringing in to mount,

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:20.300
that is the mount point
that you want to use.

00:37:20.300 --> 00:37:23.320
So all information is there.

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:26.350
And we are running the
latest version of Lustre--

00:37:26.350 --> 00:37:31.120
2.12.0 and the [INAUDIBLE] 7.6.

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:36.070
And below here is the
information that you can

00:37:36.070 --> 00:37:38.890
automatically-- we have--

00:37:38.890 --> 00:37:41.230
automate the way that
you can introduce

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:48.550
new cluster, a new client into
mounting the same file system.

00:37:48.550 --> 00:37:50.200
So you can read all that.

00:37:50.200 --> 00:37:53.370
But one interesting--
I want to show

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:56.840
you is that if you
visit the console,

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:01.300
you will see a
Lustre file system.

00:38:01.300 --> 00:38:04.290
And you see metadata.

00:38:04.290 --> 00:38:10.740
And you see the object storage
target that we just allocate.

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.630
And the total file system,
just for this demo,

00:38:13.630 --> 00:38:20.550
I just launched 25 per terabyte
with using a few minutes.

00:38:20.550 --> 00:38:27.210
And the cluster,
here are showing

00:38:27.210 --> 00:38:29.250
the OSS, which is its server.

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:33.120
